= 1
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=1}

The <cross product> of the two in-plane directions is $\mathbf u\times\mathbf v=(-5,1,2)$, with <Euclidean norm> $\sqrt{30}$. Either sign gives a <unit normal vector>:
$$
\boxed{\hat{\mathbf n}=\pm\frac{(-5,1,2)}{\sqrt{30}}}.
$$
The <plane> equation is $\hat{\mathbf n}\cdot(\mathbf r-\mathbf A)=0$, so the <distance from a point to a plane> gives
$$
\boxed{p=|\hat{\mathbf n}\cdot\mathbf A|=\frac2{\sqrt{30}}}.
$$

= 2
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Write $-8=8e^{i(\pi+2\pi k)}$. Its three roots have modulus $2$ and arguments $(\pi+2\pi k)/3$, with $k=0,1,2$. Thus all three <complex roots> are
$$
\boxed{z=1+i\sqrt3,\quad-2,\quad1-i\sqrt3}.
$$
Further integer values of $k$ repeat these roots.

= 3
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

The <Taylor series> of the logarithm gives
$$
\log(1+x^2)=x^2-\frac{x^4}{2}+O(x^6).
$$
The first nonzero term is therefore $\boxed{x^2}$. It is positive on either side of the origin, so the <stationary point> is a **<strict local minimum>**. In fact $\log(1+x^2)\geq0$, with equality only at zero, making it the <global minimum> as well.

= 4
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

The <characteristic polynomial> is $(1-\lambda)^2-4=(\lambda-3)(\lambda+1)$. For $\lambda=3$ the components are equal; for $\lambda=-1$ they are opposite. After normalization, the <eigenvalues> and associated <eigenvectors> are
$$
\boxed{\lambda_1=3,\quad\mathbf v_1=\frac{(1,1)}{\sqrt2};\qquad
\lambda_2=-1,\quad\mathbf v_2=\frac{(1,-1)}{\sqrt2}}.
$$
An overall sign change of either normalized <eigenvector> is equivalent.

= 5
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

Taking each <partial derivative> of the <scalar potential> gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbf F=\left(\cos(y^5)\sinh z,
-5xy^4\sin(y^5)\sinh z,
x\cos(y^5)\cosh z\right)}.
$$
The factor $5y^4$ in the middle component comes from the <chain rule>. The potential is smooth, so the <curl of a gradient> identity gives $\boxed{\nabla\times\mathbf F=\mathbf0}$.

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Since $\mathbf r\cdot\mathbf r=x^2+y^2+z^2$, its <gradient> is
$$
\boxed{\nabla(\mathbf r\cdot\mathbf r)=(2x,2y,2z)=2\mathbf r}.
$$

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Each Cartesian component of $a\mathbf r$ contributes $a$ to the <divergence>, while the constant vector contributes zero. Hence
$$
\boxed{\nabla\cdot(a\mathbf r-\mathbf b)=3a}.
$$

= 7
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=7}

On $x\geq1$, an <integrating factor> is $1/x$. Multiplication gives $(y/x)'=1$, so the <general solution> is $y=x^2+Cx$. The initial value $y(1)=1$ forces $C=0$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{y=x^2,\qquad x\geq1}.
$$

= 8
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=8}

The <divergence> is $y^2+x^2+z^2=r^2$. In <spherical coordinates>, the angular integral is $4\pi$ and the <volume element> contributes $r^2\,dr$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\iiint_{r\leq R}\nabla\cdot\mathbf F\,dV
=4\pi\int_0^R r^4\,dr=\frac{4\pi R^5}{5}}.
$$

= 9
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=9}

The original PDF contains the product $\sin x(1+4\cos x)$; the transcription drops the $x$ after the first <sine>. Using $2\sin x\cos x=\sin2x$ gives the finite <Fourier sine series>
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\sin x+2\sin2x}.
$$
Thus $b_1=1$, $b_2=2$, and all other <sine> coefficients vanish. No <cosine> or constant terms are present.

= 10
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Treat the fair coin tosses as independent. Multiplying the ten head <probabilities> gives
$$
\boxed{P(10\text{ heads})=\left(\frac12\right)^{10}}.
$$

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The number of tails has a <binomial distribution>. The complement of at least two tails consists of zero tails or exactly one tail. Therefore
$$
\boxed{P(\text{at least two tails})
=1-\frac{\binom{10}{0}+\binom{10}{1}}{2^{10}}
=1-\frac{11}{1024}}.
$$

= 11
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Equating the three Cartesian components gives
$$
2+\lambda=-\mu,\qquad -1+\lambda=6+2\mu,\qquad
\lambda=4+\mu.
$$
The first and third equations yield $\lambda+\mu=-2$ and $\lambda-\mu=4$, hence $\lambda=1$, $\mu=-3$. They also satisfy the middle equation, so the intersection is
$$
\boxed{\lambda=1,\quad\mu=-3,\quad\mathbf p=(3,0,1)}.
$$

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Set $s=\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf r$. Taking the <dot product> of the vector equation with $\mathbf a$ gives
$$
(1+\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b)s=\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf c.
$$
Here $\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b=5$ and $\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf c=6$, so $s=1$. Substitution then gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbf r=\mathbf c-\mathbf b=(1,0,0)}.
$$
Geometrically the possible vector must lie on the line $\mathbf c-s\mathbf b$, and the scalar <constraint> picks one point. Equivalently the <rank-one matrix update> $I+\mathbf b\mathbf a^T$ is invertible because $1+\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b=6\ne0$. This is the nonsingular case of <solvability of an affine rank-one vector equation>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The original PDF has $\mathbf c=(-2,1,1)$ here; the transcription loses its minus sign. Now $\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b=-1$ and $\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf c=0$, so the scalar condition becomes $0=0$ and leaves $s$ arbitrary. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\mathbf r=\mathbf c-s\mathbf b=(-2-s,1+s,1+s),\qquad s\in\mathbb R}.
$$
The <solution set> is a straight line parallel to $\mathbf b$, equivalently the intersection of $y=z$ and $x+y=-1$.

For the geometric mechanism, let $L=I+\mathbf b\mathbf a^T$. Since $\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b=-1$, $L^2=L$, $L\mathbf b=0$, and $\mathbf a\cdot L\mathbf r=0$. Thus $L$ is an <oblique projection> onto the <plane> $\mathbf a^\perp$ along $\mathbf b$. The specified $\mathbf c$ lies in this image <plane>, and adding any multiple of the <kernel> direction $\mathbf b$ leaves its image unchanged.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The same scalar consistency condition now has $\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf c=1$, so it reads $0=1$. Hence **there is no solution**.

Geometrically the image of $I+\mathbf b\mathbf a^T$ is still the <plane> $\mathbf a^\perp$, while this $\mathbf c$ is outside it. Thus the failure is inconsistency, rather than a unique solution with a singular denominator.

= 12
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Orient the common axis of the wedge faces along $z$ and its azimuthal bisector along positive $x$. Reflection symmetries give $\bar y=\bar z=0$. In <spherical coordinates>, use $0\leq r\leq a$, $0\leq\theta\leq\pi$, and $-\beta/2\leq\varphi\leq\beta/2$. The <volume> is
$$
V=\int_0^a r^2\,dr\int_0^\pi\sin\theta\,d\theta\int_{-\beta/2}^{\beta/2}d\varphi
=\frac{2\beta a^3}{3}.
$$
Since $x=r\sin\theta\cos\varphi$, the first moment is
$$
\int_Vx\,dV=\left(\frac{a^4}{4}\right)
\left(\int_0^\pi\sin^2\theta\,d\theta\right)
\left(\int_{-\beta/2}^{\beta/2}\cos\varphi\,d\varphi\right)
=\frac{\pi a^4}{4}\sin\frac\beta2.
$$
Dividing by the <volume> locates the <centre of mass>:
$$
\boxed{\bar{\mathbf x}=\frac{3\pi a}{8\beta}\sin\frac\beta2\,\mathbf e_x},\qquad
\boxed{f(\beta)=\frac{3\pi}{8\beta}\sin\frac\beta2}.
$$
This is the centroid formula for a <spherical wedge>.

For the sketch, $f$ is positive and decreases from $\lim_{\beta\to0^+}f=3\pi/16$ to $\lim_{\beta\to2\pi^-}f=0$, with $f(\pi)=3/8$. Indeed, on putting $q=\beta/2$, the sign of $f'$ is the sign of $q\cos q-\sin q$. The negative of this expression has <derivative> $q\sin q>0$ for $0<q<\pi$ and starts at zero, proving strict decrease.

\Image[paper-2-spherical-wedge-centroid.png]
{title=Distance of the centre of mass of a spherical wedge from its sphere centre}

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the same axes as in (a) and choose the outward orientation. On the curved <spherical lune>, $\mathbf n=\mathbf e_r$ and $dA=a^2\sin\theta\,d\theta\,d\varphi$. Its <vector area> is
$$
\mathbf S=a^2\int_{-\beta/2}^{\beta/2}\int_0^\pi
(\sin\theta\cos\varphi,\sin\theta\sin\varphi,\cos\theta)
\sin\theta\,d\theta\,d\varphi.
$$
The $y$ and $z$ components cancel. The $x$ component is $a^2(\pi/2)\,2\sin(\beta/2)$, hence
$$
\boxed{\mathbf S=\pi a^2\sin\frac\beta2\,\mathbf e_x}.
$$
Here $\mathbf e_x$ points along the azimuthal bisector; another coordinate choice rotates this vector accordingly. Its magnitude is the signed projected <area>, not the scalar curved <area> $2\beta a^2$. Reversing the orientation reverses its sign.

= 13
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <curl> of the first <vector field> is
$$
\nabla\times\mathbf F=(0,-xy\sinh z,x\cosh z),
$$
which is not identically zero. Thus **$\mathbf F$ is not conservative** on $\mathbb R^3$ and cannot equal a negative <gradient> there.

For the second field, direct differentiation shows
$$
\mathbf G=\nabla(x^2y\cosh z).
$$
Therefore **$\mathbf G$ is conservative**, and the requested negative-gradient potential is
$$
\boxed{\Phi=-x^2y\cosh z+C},\qquad\mathbf G=-\nabla\Phi.
$$
Its <curl> vanishes, consistently with the <curl of a gradient> identity.

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

On the first straight segment, $y=z=0$ and only $dx$ is nonzero, so both integrals vanish. On the second segment, $x=1,z=0$ and only $dy$ is nonzero; each integrand is one, contributing $1$. On the last segment, $x=y=1$ and only $dz$ is nonzero; each integrand is $\sinh z$, contributing $\cosh1-1$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\int_C\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf x=\cosh1,\qquad
\int_C\mathbf G\cdot d\mathbf x=\cosh1}.
$$
The second result also follows from the <fundamental theorem for line integrals>, using the potential found in (a). Agreement on this one path does not make $\mathbf F$ conservative.

= c
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Parametrize the diagonal by $\mathbf x(t)=(t,t,t)$ for $0\leq t\leq1$. Then
$$
\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf x'=2t^2\cosh t+t^3\sinh t,
\qquad \mathbf G\cdot\mathbf x'=3t^2\cosh t+t^3\sinh t.
$$
The second expression is $(t^3\cosh t)'$, immediately giving $\int\mathbf G\cdot d\mathbf x=\cosh1$. The first differs from it by $t^2\cosh t$. Two applications of <integration by parts> give
$$
\int_0^1t^2\cosh t\,dt
=\left[t^2\sinh t-2t\cosh t+2\sinh t\right]_0^1
=3\sinh1-2\cosh1.
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{\int_C\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf x=3(\cosh1-\sinh1)=\frac3e},\qquad
\boxed{\int_C\mathbf G\cdot d\mathbf x=\cosh1}.
$$
The changed value for $\mathbf F$ explicitly demonstrates path dependence, while $\mathbf G$ gives the same endpoint-dependent result.

= 14
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A <uniform distribution> has constant density on its support, fixed by normalization. Thus
$$
\boxed{f_1(t)=\begin{cases}1/T_1,&0<t<T_1,\\0,&\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}}
$$
The density's assigned values at the endpoints do not affect <probabilities>.

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Normalization requires $1=\int_0^{T_2}At\,dt=AT_2^2/2$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{A=\frac2{T_2^2},\qquad f_2(t)=\begin{cases}2t/T_2^2,&0<t<T_2,\\0,&\text{otherwise}.\end{cases}}
$$
The graph rises linearly from zero to the left-hand limit $2/T_2$, then drops to zero beyond $T_2$. Its enclosed triangular <area> is one. The combined sketch in (f) uses dimensionless time and shows this density together with the cumulative <probabilities>.

= c
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Integrating the first <probability density function> gives the <cumulative distribution function>
$$
\boxed{P_1(t)=\begin{cases}
0,&t\leq0,\\t/T_1,&0<t<T_1,\\1,&t\geq T_1.
\end{cases}}
$$
Its graph is a straight ramp from $(0,0)$ to $(T_1,1)$, followed by a horizontal plateau.

= d
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The second <cumulative distribution function> is the integral of its density:
$$
\boxed{P_2(t)=\begin{cases}
0,&t\leq0,\\t^2/T_2^2,&0<t<T_2,\\1,&t\geq T_2.
\end{cases}}
$$
Its graph is an upward-curving parabolic arc from $(0,0)$ to $(T_2,1)$, followed by a horizontal plateau. Both cumulative functions are continuous at their upper support endpoints.

= e
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

**The product formula requires independent failure times.** This assumption is not stated in the original PDF. Under that intended assumption, put $s=t/T$ and use the <failure probabilities for two independent lifetimes>:
$$
\boxed{P(t)=P_1(t)P_2(t)=\begin{cases}
0,&t\leq0,\\s^3,&0<t<T,\\1,&t\geq T.
\end{cases}}
$$
Its graph is a cubic increasing from $(0,0)$ to $(T,1)$, convex before reaching its plateau.

Without independence, the marginal distributions do not determine $P(t)$. The <Fréchet inequalities> give the sharp pointwise bounds
$$
\boxed{\max(0,s+s^2-1)\leq P(t)\leq s^2,\qquad0<s<1}.
$$
For example, taking $t_1=TU$ and $t_2=T\sqrt U$ with the same uniform random variable $U$ preserves both prescribed marginals but gives $P(t)=s^2$, rather than $s^3$.

= f
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

Under the same independence assumption, <inclusion-exclusion> gives
$$
\boxed{R(t)=P_1(t)+P_2(t)-P(t)=\begin{cases}
0,&t\leq0,\\s+s^2-s^3,&0<t<T,\\1,&t\geq T.
\end{cases}}
$$
On $0<t<T$,
$$
R'(t)=\frac{1+2s-3s^2}{T}=\frac{(1-s)(3s+1)}T>0,
\qquad R''(t)=\frac{2-6s}{T^2}.
$$
There is one interior <inflection point>, where the <curvature> changes from positive to negative:
$$
\boxed{\left(t,R(t)\right)=\left(\frac T3,\frac{11}{27}\right)}.
$$
At $t=T$ the graph reaches one with zero slope and then stays constant. There is no further change from one sign of <curvature> to the opposite sign there.

\Image[paper-2-failure-probabilities.png]
{title=Failure densities and cumulative probabilities, with independent lifetimes for the joint curves}

Without independence, $R=P_1+P_2-P$ still holds, but only $\boxed{s\leq R(t)\leq\min(1,s+s^2)}$ is forced by the marginals. Its inflection behaviour is then not determined. The shared-$U$ construction in (e), for example, gives $R=s$ before the plateau.

= 15
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <characteristic equation> has roots $-2,-6$, and a trial multiple of $e^{-4t}$ gives the <particular solution> $-3e^{-4t}$. Thus
$$
f(t)=Ae^{-2t}+Be^{-6t}-3e^{-4t}.
$$
The first two conditions give $A+B=3$ and $-2A-6B+12=0$, hence $A=B=3/2$. At $t_*=\log\sqrt2$, the exponentials $e^{-2t_*},e^{-4t_*},e^{-6t_*}$ equal $1/2,1/4,1/8$. Therefore
$$
f(t_*)=\frac34+\frac3{16}-\frac34=\frac3{16}\ne0.
$$
**No solution satisfies all three conditions.** The first two initial data already determine a unique solution of the <second-order linear differential equation>; the extra condition is inconsistent with it.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Use the same <general solution>. The first two conditions now give $A+B=3$ and $-2A-6B+12=-2$, so $A=1$, $B=2$. Hence
$$
\boxed{f(t)=e^{-2t}-3e^{-4t}+2e^{-6t}}.
$$
At $t=\log\sqrt2$, its value is $1/2-3/4+2/8=0$, so the third condition is also satisfied. This is the unique consistent solution.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The repeated factor in the <differential operator> suggests $y=e^{-x}u(x)$. Direct differentiation gives $y''+2y'+y=e^{-x}u''$, so $u''=4$ and
$$
u=2x^2+Bx+C.
$$
The first value gives $C=1$. The second gives $u(1)=ey(1)=1$, hence $2+B+1=1$ and $B=-2$. Therefore
$$
y(x)=e^{-x}(2x^2-2x+1),\qquad \boxed{y(2)=5e^{-2}}.
$$

= 16
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a continuously differentiable <differential one-form>, exactness requires $\boxed{P_y=Q_x}$. It is locally sufficient, and globally sufficient on a <simply connected domain>; on a domain with holes, closedness alone need not imply exactness.

An <integrating factor for a differential one-form> depending only on $x$ must satisfy
$$
\partial_y(\mu P)=\partial_x(\mu Q),\qquad
\mu P_y=\mu'Q+\mu Q_x.
$$
Thus, where $Q\ne0$,
$$
\boxed{\frac{\mu'}\mu=\frac{P_y-Q_x}{Q},\qquad
\mu(x)=\exp\int\frac{P_y-Q_x}{Q}\,dx}.
$$
If the ratio depends only on $x$, this defines a nonzero factor and makes it a <closed differential form>, hence locally exact. For a factor depending only on $y$, the analogous calculation gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{\mu'}\mu=\frac{Q_x-P_y}{P},\qquad
\mu(y)=\exp\int\frac{Q_x-P_y}{P}\,dy},
$$
provided the ratio is a <function> only of $y$ on the region where $P\ne0$. A nonzero constant multiple of either factor works equally well.

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Write the equation as $P\,dx+Q\,dy=0$, with $P=3x+y$ and $Q=2x$. Then
$$
\frac{P_y-Q_x}{Q}=\frac{1-2}{2x}=-\frac1{2x},
$$
so a suitable <integrating factor> is $\mu=|x|^{-1/2}$ on either half-line. For example, on $x>0$ the resulting exact form is
$$
\left(3\sqrt x+\frac y{\sqrt x}\right)dx+2\sqrt x\,dy
=d\left[2\sqrt x(x+y)\right].
$$
Hence the solution, written to cover either sign of $x$, is
$$
\boxed{y=-x+\frac{C}{\sqrt{|x|}},\qquad x\ne0}.
$$
The original equation remains meaningful at $x=0$, where it requires $y=0$. Only $C=0$ gives a finite differentiable extension across zero, namely $y=-x$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Here $P=y^2$ and $Q=\cos^2x+y\sin2x$. Their <derivatives> give
$$
P_y-Q_x=2y+\sin2x-2y\cos2x=2\tan x\,Q.
$$
Thus on intervals with $\cos x\ne0$, choose the <integrating factor> $\mu=e^{\int2\tan x\,dx}=\sec^2x$. The multiplied form is
$$
y^2\sec^2x\,dx+(1+2y\tan x)\,dy
=d(y+y^2\tan x).
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{y+y^2\tan x=C},\qquad\cos x\ne0.
$$
This implicit family includes $y=0$ and the branch $y=-\cot x$ when $C=0$. Where the coefficient of $y'$ is nonzero, its differentiated form directly recovers the original equation. A useful equivalent relation is $y\cos x+y^2\sin x=C\cos x$. At $\cos x=0$, the original equation requires $y=0$; continuation there must be checked in that original equation rather than justified by the singular <integrating factor>. In particular the zero solution extends everywhere, and $y=-\cot x$ extends smoothly across the <cosine> zeros where it is defined.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Put $y=ux$, so $y'=u+xu'$. On an interval with $x\ne0$, substitution and cancellation give
$$
x(u-1)\frac{du}{dx}=-(u^2+2u+2).
$$
Since $u^2+2u+2=(u+1)^2+1>0$, no constant-$u$ solution is lost when separating. Integrate
$$
\int\frac{u-1}{(u+1)^2+1}\,du=-\int\frac{dx}{x}
$$
to obtain
$$
\frac12\log((u+1)^2+1)-2\arctan(u+1)=-\log|x|+C.
$$
After restoring $u=y/x$, a local implicit <general solution> is
$$
\boxed{\frac12\log\left(x^2+(x+y)^2\right)
-2\arctan\left(\frac{x+y}{x}\right)=C}.
$$
The inverse tangent is chosen continuously on a solution branch. Using the continuous polar angle of $(x,x+y)$ extends the expression to charts through $x=0$ when the original equation permits it. A differentiable graph cannot pass through $y=x\ne0$, because there the original equation would require $5x=0$.

= 17
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

An <eigenvalue equation> has the form $A\mathbf a=\lambda\mathbf a$, where $\mathbf a\ne0$ is an <eigenvector> and $\lambda$ its <eigenvalue>. It means that the <linear map> preserves the vector's line and acts there by multiplication by $\lambda$. Nonzero solutions occur precisely when $\det(A-\lambda I)=0$; the zero vector is excluded because it would satisfy the equation for every $\lambda$.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Each proposed displacement has second <derivative> $\ddot x_i=-\omega^2a_i\cos\omega t$. Matching the coefficients of the common time factor in the three equations gives
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
m\omega^2a_1&=k(2a_1-a_2),\\
m\omega^2a_2&=k(-a_1+2a_2-a_3),\\
m\omega^2a_3&=k(-a_2+2a_3).
\end{aligned}}
$$
These algebraic relations hold for the entire oscillation, including the isolated times when the common <cosine> vanishes.

= c
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

With $\mathbf a=(a_1,a_2,a_3)^T$, the <eigenvalue equation> is
$$
\boxed{D\mathbf a=\lambda\mathbf a,\qquad
D=\begin{pmatrix}2&-1&0\\-1&2&-1\\0&-1&2\end{pmatrix},\qquad
\lambda=\frac{m\omega^2}{k}}.
$$
The dimensionless matrix $D$ is the <stiffness matrix> divided by $k$; its <eigenvalues> determine squared angular frequencies.

= d
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Expanding the <determinant> along the first row gives
$$
\det(D-\lambda I)=(2-\lambda)\bigl[(2-\lambda)^2-2\bigr].
$$
The <eigenvalues> and normalized spatial <eigenvectors>, in increasing frequency order, are
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c|c}
\lambda_j&\mathbf a^{(j)}&\omega_j^2\\\hline
2-\sqrt2&\tfrac12(1,\sqrt2,1)&(k/m)(2-\sqrt2)\\
2&\tfrac1{\sqrt2}(1,0,-1)&2k/m\\
2+\sqrt2&\tfrac12(1,-\sqrt2,1)&(k/m)(2+\sqrt2)
\end{array}}.
$$
For each mode both signs of $\omega_j$ give the same <cosine> displacement. An arbitrary overall mode amplitude remains free. These vectors are the $N=3$ instances of the <sine eigenvectors of the fixed-end spring chain>.

= e
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The pairwise <dot products> of the normalized vectors are
$$
\frac{1-1}{2\sqrt2}=0,\qquad
\frac{1-2+1}{4}=0,\qquad
\frac{1-1}{2\sqrt2}=0.
$$
Their squared norms are respectively $(1+2+1)/4$, $(1+1)/2$, and $(1+2+1)/4$, all one. Hence they form an **<orthonormal basis>**. This also follows from the <spectral theorem for real symmetric matrices>, because $D$ is symmetric and its three <eigenvalues> are distinct.

= f
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

The largest angular-frequency magnitude is $\omega_3=\sqrt{(2+\sqrt2)k/m}$. With overall amplitude $A$, the corresponding displacements are
$$
\boxed{x_1=x_3=\frac A2\cos(\omega_3t),\qquad
x_2=-\frac A{\sqrt2}\cos(\omega_3t)}.
$$
Thus the two outer masses move together, while the middle <mass> moves oppositely with $\sqrt2$ times their amplitude. All three have the same period $2\pi/\omega_3$.

\Image[paper-2-highest-spring-mode.png]
{title=Highest-frequency mode of three equal masses between fixed supports}

= 18
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

One period advances the fundamental phase by $2\pi$, so the <angular frequency> is
$$
\boxed{\omega=\frac{2\pi}{T}}.
$$

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <sine> and <cosine> functions are orthogonal over a complete period. For positive integers $m,n$,
$$
\int_0^T\cos(m\omega t)\cos(n\omega t)\,dt
=\int_0^T\sin(m\omega t)\sin(n\omega t)\,dt
=\frac T2\delta_{mn},
$$
while every mixed sine-cosine integral and every integral of a nonconstant single harmonic is zero. These identities follow from the product-to-sum formulas and the integer-period endpoint values.

Multiply the <Fourier series> by $\cos(m\omega t)$ and integrate. All terms except $a_m\cos^2(m\omega t)$ vanish, giving $Ta_m/2$. Repeating with <sine> isolates $b_m$. Thus
$$
\boxed{a_n=\frac2T\int_0^T V_1(t)\cos(n\omega t)\,dt,\qquad
b_n=\frac2T\int_0^T V_1(t)\sin(n\omega t)\,dt},\quad n\geq1.
$$
Integrating without a harmonic also gives $a_0=(2/T)\int_0^T V_1(t)\,dt$. The factor $1/2$ on the constant term in the chosen convention is essential.

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Put $s=\omega t$, so the nonzero half-cycle is $0\leq s\leq\pi$. The constant coefficient is
$$
a_0=\frac{V_0}{\pi}\int_0^\pi\sin s\,ds=\frac{2V_0}{\pi}.
$$
The <sine> coefficients satisfy
$$
b_n=\frac{V_0}{\pi}\int_0^\pi\sin s\sin ns\,ds
=\begin{cases}V_0/2,&n=1,\\0,&n\ne1,\end{cases}
$$
by <sine> <orthogonality>. For the <cosine> coefficients, the product-to-sum identity gives, when $n\ne1$,
$$
\int_0^\pi\sin s\cos ns\,ds
=\frac12\int_0^\pi[\sin((1+n)s)+\sin((1-n)s)]\,ds
=\frac{1+(-1)^n}{1-n^2}.
$$
The case $n=1$ is zero directly. Hence
$$
\boxed{a_{2m}=-\frac{2V_0}{\pi(4m^2-1)},\quad a_{2m-1}=0,\quad
b_1=\frac{V_0}{2},\quad b_n=0\ (n\ne1)}.
$$
The first five nonzero terms, counting the constant as the zero-frequency term, are
$$
\boxed{V_1(t)=\frac{V_0}{\pi}+\frac{V_0}{2}\sin\omega t
-\frac{2V_0}{3\pi}\cos2\omega t
-\frac{2V_0}{15\pi}\cos4\omega t
-\frac{2V_0}{35\pi}\cos6\omega t+\cdots}.
$$
The general coefficient formula specifies every remaining term. This is the <Fourier series of a half-wave rectified sinusoid>; the waveform is continuous at the joins, so the series gives its actual value there.

= d
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The given identity immediately yields $V_2=V_1-V_0\sin\omega t$. Thus only the fundamental <sine> coefficient changes: $V_0/2$ becomes $-V_0/2$. No new integral is needed. The first five nonzero terms are
$$
\boxed{V_2(t)=\frac{V_0}{\pi}-\frac{V_0}{2}\sin\omega t
-\frac{2V_0}{3\pi}\cos2\omega t
-\frac{2V_0}{15\pi}\cos4\omega t
-\frac{2V_0}{35\pi}\cos6\omega t+\cdots}.
$$
This also follows from <translation of a Fourier series>: a half-period delay multiplies the $n$th harmonic by $(-1)^n$, leaving the constant and even harmonics unchanged and reversing the only odd harmonic present.

= 19
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

At a regular constrained <stationary point>, the change in $f$ along every allowed tangent direction must vanish. Thus its <gradient> is normal to the <constraint> curve, giving the <Lagrange multiplier> equations
$$
\boxed{\nabla f=\lambda\nabla g,\qquad g=c},
$$
provided $\nabla g\ne0$. Solve these equations for $x,y,\lambda$, then classify the candidates using the <constraint> or a constrained second-derivative test. Points where the <constraint> <gradient> vanishes require separate analysis.

For $n$ variables and $r$ regular independent constraints $g_j=c_j$, use one multiplier per <constraint>:
$$
\boxed{\nabla f=\sum_{j=1}^r\lambda_j\nabla g_j,\qquad g_j=c_j}.
$$
There are $n+r$ scalar equations in the $n+r$ unknowns. Independence of the <constraint> gradients is the regularity condition; boundary points must be treated separately when present.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use $\mathcal L=-\sum_i x_i\log x_i-\lambda(\sum_i x_i-1)$. Differentiating with respect to each positive $x_i$ gives
$$
-\log x_i-1-\lambda=0.
$$
Every $x_i$ is therefore equal; normalization forces
$$
\boxed{x_i=\frac1n\quad\text{for all }i},\qquad
\boxed{f_{\rm stationary}=\log n}.
$$
The <Hessian matrix> of $f$ is diagonal with entries $-1/x_i$, so its quadratic form is strictly negative for every nonzero perturbation. In particular it is negative along the tangent space $\sum_i\delta x_i=0$, making this the unique global maximum on the positive <probability simplex>. This is the <maximum entropy distribution on a finite set>, using natural logarithms.

= c
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Introduce a second multiplier $b$ and use
$$
\mathcal L=-\sum_i x_i\log x_i-\lambda\left(\sum_i x_i-1\right)
-b\left(\sum_i x_i y_i-Y\right).
$$
The stationary equations become $-\log x_i-1-\lambda-by_i=0$. Writing $a=e^{-1-\lambda}>0$ gives
$$
\boxed{x_i=ae^{-by_i}}.
$$
The two constraints determine the constants through
$$
\boxed{a\sum_{i=1}^n e^{-by_i}=1,\qquad
a\sum_{i=1}^n y_i e^{-by_i}=Y}.
$$
Equivalently, $a=1/Z(b)$ with $Z(b)=\sum_i e^{-by_i}$, and $-d\log Z/db=Y$. This is the <maximum entropy distribution with a prescribed finite mean>.

For nonconstant $y_i$, a strictly positive solution exists exactly when $\min_i y_i<Y<\max_i y_i$. The mean's <derivative> with respect to $b$ is $-\operatorname{Var}_b(y)<0$, so $b$ is unique in that case. At either extreme mean no strictly positive solution exists. If all $y_i=y_0$, feasibility requires $Y=y_0$ and the extra <constraint> is redundant: $x_i=1/n$, while $a,b$ are not separately identifiable. The negative entropy Hessian ensures that any feasible interior <stationary point> is the unique constrained maximum.

= 20
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

In a steady state, $\nu u_s''=-G\sin(n\pi x)$. Since the second <derivative> of the <sine> is $-(n\pi)^2$ times itself,
$$
u_s(x)=\frac{G}{\nu n^2\pi^2}\sin(n\pi x)+Ax+B.
$$
The zero boundary values at $x=0,1$ force $A=B=0$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{u_s(x)=\frac{G}{\nu n^2\pi^2}\sin(n\pi x)}.
$$

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The steady profile has no time <derivative>. Substituting $u=\widetilde u+u_s$ and using its steady equation cancels the forcing, giving
$$
\boxed{\widetilde u_t=\nu\widetilde u_{xx},\qquad
\widetilde u(0,t)=\widetilde u(1,t)=0}.
$$
The initial condition is
$$
\boxed{\widetilde u(x,0)=-u_s(x)=-\frac{G}{\nu n^2\pi^2}\sin(n\pi x)}.
$$
The resulting <heat equation> is independent of $G$, as are its <boundary conditions>. Its initial amplitude still depends on $G$; removing a source from the evolution equation does not remove it from the whole problem.

= c
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Separate $\widetilde u=f(t)g(x)$. On a nonzero product region,
$$
\frac{f'}{\nu f}=\frac{g''}{g}=-\lambda.
$$
A decaying temporal factor requires $\lambda>0$, with $f=Ce^{-\nu\lambda t}$. The zero spatial boundary values give $g=A\sin(\sqrt\lambda x)$ and $\sin\sqrt\lambda=0$, hence $\lambda=j^2\pi^2$ with $j=1,2,\ldots$. Zero or negative separation parameters give no nonzero spatial mode with both boundary values zero.

The initial profile is already exactly the $j=n$ <sine> <eigenfunction>. Its coefficient therefore determines a single separated solution:
$$
\boxed{\widetilde u(x,t)=-\frac{G}{\nu n^2\pi^2}
e^{-\nu n^2\pi^2t}\sin(n\pi x)}.
$$
The exponential tends to zero because $\nu>0$. More generally <sine> <orthogonality> would determine a sum of such modes, but all other coefficients vanish here. This is the transient of the <heat equation with a single stationary sine forcing>.

= d
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Adding the steady and transient parts gives
$$
\boxed{u(x,t)=\frac{G}{\nu n^2\pi^2}
\left(1-e^{-\nu n^2\pi^2t}\right)\sin(n\pi x)}.
$$
Integrating the <sine> across the channel gives $\int_0^1\sin(n\pi x)\,dx=[1-(-1)^n]/(n\pi)$. Thus
$$
\boxed{Q(t)=\frac{G[1-(-1)^n]}{\nu n^3\pi^3}
\left(1-e^{-\nu n^2\pi^2t}\right)}.
$$
For even $n$ the signed flow rate is zero, because positive and negative velocity lobes cancel. For odd $n$ the bracket is two. Both expressions vanish initially and approach their steady values exponentially; $G=0$ gives the trivial zero solution throughout.
